[telegram] Message from 8385420240: This is what claude code and i are setting up right now for claude code. So how does this affect you. We are trying to se set up a safely autonomous machine here. These passwords are all for accounts set up for you 

 Option 1: Passwordless sudo (most common for autonomous agents)                                                 
  Add specific commands to /etc/sudoers so they run without a password. You control exactly which commands are    
  allowed. Most people do this for specific tools, not blanket sudo.                                              
                  
  Option 2: Store password, pass it via -S flag
  sudo -S reads the password from stdin. You could store the password in a secure location (like a local .env file
   or macOS Keychain) and pipe it in. Less elegant but works.

  Option 3: NOPASSWD for all sudo
  Full passwordless sudo. Convenient but reduces security — if anything compromises this machine, it has full root
   access.

  What I'd recommend for this setup:
  Since this is a dedicated agent machine (not your daily driver), Option 1 is the right call — add specific
  commands to sudoers that Nico/Claude Code legitimately needs, like enabling/disabling services. Everything else
  still requires your password.

