Best Practices
Block Ownership (Critical)
| Block | Primary Writes | Sleeptime Writes | Both Read |
|-------|---------------|------------------|-----------|
| persona | ✅ | ❌ | ✅ |
| human | ✅ | ❌ | ✅ |
| project_context | ✅ | ❌ | ✅ |
| pending_items | ✅ | ❌ | ✅ |
| coordination | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ |
| guidance | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ |
| patterns | ✅ (explicit) | ✅ (observed) | ✅ |

Avoid Concurrent Writes
Cameron's pattern: Use memory_insert or delegation
# Instead of both editing same block:
# Sleeptime writes to coordination block
# Primary reads coordination, moves to project_context
Use Read-Only Flag Strategically
# Mark blocks as read_only=True for sleeptime blocks
# Prevents accidental edits during concurrent access
block = client.blocks.create(
    label="coordination",
    value="...",
    read_only=True  # Sleeptime writes via special handling
)
The Subconscious Migration Pattern
From your earlier setup (which follows Cameron's pattern):
Old (Claude Code + Subconscious):
  Claude Code ──► Subconscious agent (observes after)
      │                │
      └────────────────┘
           memories

New (Letta Code Primary + Sleeptime):
  Primary Agent ──► Sleeptime Agent (processes after)
      │                  │
      └──────────────────┘
        Shared memory blocks
Key difference: Letta native vs external system

Summary
| Aspect | Cameron's Recommendation |
|--------|-------------------------|
| Shared blocks | Yes, for coordination and guidance |
| Read-only strategy | Mark sleeptime output blocks as read-only |
| Tool variables | Expose both agent IDs to tools |
| Concurrency | Avoid simultaneous writes, use append |
| Sleeptime writes to | coordination, guidance blocks |
| Primary reads from | Shared blocks, writes to context blocks |

The core insight: Sleeptime is a background processor that updates shared memory, primary is the conversational interface that reads those updates. Both share context but have distinct responsibilities. 🧠[39m
